[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Middleware Pipeline and Request Lifecycle in .NET"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the request lifecycle and middleware pipeline is fundamental to building robust web applications in .NET. Middleware components are assemblies that are wired into the application pipeline to handle requests and responses."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is the Middleware Pipeline?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The .NET request pipeline is composed of a sequence of middleware delegates, often referred to as the onion model. Each piece of middleware can perform operations before and after the next delegate in the pipeline, as well as decide whether to pass the request to the next component."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Middleware in Program.cs"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern .NET applications, the pipeline is configured sequentially in the Program.cs file using the WebApplication builder. The order of registration matters significantly."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
var app = builder.Build();

// 1. Exception handling middleware
app.UseExceptionHandler("/Error");

// 2. HTTPS redirection middleware
app.UseHttpsRedirection();

// 3. Static files middleware
app.UseStaticFiles();

// 4. Routing middleware
app.UseRouting();

// 5. Authentication and Authorization
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();

// 6. Endpoint routing execution
app.MapControllers();

app.Run();"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Custom Middleware"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can write custom middleware to handle cross-cutting concerns such as logging, performance monitoring, or custom header injection. A custom middleware class typically accepts a RequestDelegate in its constructor and defines an InvokeAsync method."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public class RequestTimingMiddleware
{
    readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    readonly ILogger<RequestTimingMiddleware> _logger;

    public RequestTimingMiddleware(RequestDelegate next, ILogger<RequestTimingMiddleware> logger)
    {
        _next = next;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
    {
        var watch = System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch.StartNew();

        try
        {
            await _next(context);
        }
        finally
        {
            watch.Stop();
            _logger.LogInformation("Request {Path} took {ElapsedMilliseconds}ms", context.Request.Path, watch.ElapsedMilliseconds);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Extension Method for Clean Registration"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"It is a best practice to wrap custom middleware registration inside an IApplicationBuilder extension method for cleaner Program.cs configuration."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public static class RequestTimingMiddlewareExtensions
{
    public static IApplicationBuilder UseRequestTiming(this IApplicationBuilder builder)
    {
        return builder.UseMiddleware<RequestTimingMiddleware>();
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Takeaways"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Middleware execution order in Program.cs is strictly sequential; register exception handlers and security headers first."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use app.Use() for custom inline middleware logic, or dedicated classes for complex components."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always invoke the next delegate (await _next(context)) unless you intentionally want to short-circuit the request."}]
